## {#today .darkslide background-color="rgba(0,0,0,0)"}

::: {.todaymeta}
A one-day course · four parts of 90 minutes · the cohabitation case
:::

:::: {.todaybox}
::: {.nonincremental .fs-85}
1. Credibility and tools – science, its crisis, and the toolchain
2. The claim, its estimand and its causal graph – the paper, the five analysts, and who cohabits at all
3. Reproduction and exploration – a recorded result reproduced and questioned, and the specification menu
4. Multiverse, replication and preregistration – the curve, the two replications, one preregistered analysis
:::
::::

::: {.todaytake .fs-85}
You leave with: a reproduced result, and what it rests on · an estimand statement and a causal graph of your own · a preregistered analysis · your results on a live class multiverse
:::

::: {.todaymeta}
Work along in your IDE (Positron, RStudio, VS Code, etc.), solo or in pairs, with the browser lab page as the fallback
:::

::: notes
Welcome – good to have you here. A full day, in four parts of ninety minutes. The first part sets up everything else: what we are doing when we do social science, why people say it is in crisis, what open research proposes to do about it, and the software without which none of it happens. The second part goes inside one published study, built on the National Survey of Family Growth, which asked whether American couples who live together unmarried have become less likely to marry, and asks what question its claim actually answers. In the third you reproduce a result that one of five independent analysts reported for that claim, find out what it rests on, and then vary the analysis yourself. And in the fourth we read what the class built, look at two replications, and settle on the one analysis you would publish.
:::

## Two kinds of aim {.smaller}

:::: {.columns}
::: {.column width="50%"}
::: {.small}
**What I want you to understand**

- What reproducibility, robustness and replicability each mean, and why they come apart
- Why analysts disagree – and how much of that is disagreement about the *question*
- What a causal graph is good for, in an experiment as much as in observational data
- What a multiverse shows, and what it cannot show
- What preregistration settles, and what it leaves open
:::
:::
::: {.column width="50%"}
::: {.small}
**What I want you to be able to do**

- Get an analysis running from someone else's materials
- Write an estimand statement, and a causal graph that matches it
- Reproduce a published result exactly, and explain why it came out as it did
- Vary a specification one choice at a time and read what changed
- Preregister an analysis before you run it, and report where it sits among the alternatives
:::
:::
::::

::: notes
Two columns, because the course has two kinds of aim. On the left is what I want you to understand, and most of it is conceptual work that has little to do with code. On the right is what I want you to be able to do, and every item there is something you will have done at least once with your own hands before we finish. If you take home only the left-hand column, you will have heard some interesting arguments and changed nothing about how you work. If you take home only the right-hand column, you will have learned some R. What I am after is the two together, because the concepts tell you which of the things you can now do is worth doing.
:::

