## The 1995 rows of the file {#the-1995-rows-of-the-file .smaller}

::: {.small}
- From 2002 the NSFG releases a recode, COH1DUR, with the length of the first cohabitation. The 1995 file has none, so the length has to be built from the dates of the history of each woman
- The data dictionary of the SCORE replication builds it as **"STOPOTH0 - STRTOTH0"**: the month in which the woman stopped living with her first former partner, minus the month in which she began to
- That date exists only for a partner with whom she **no longer lived**. The rule is right for almost every union that dissolved, and gives no length to a union that became a marriage or was still intact
- Of the 929 unions of the paper, the file holds **346 of the 347 separations, 2 of the 340 marriages and 3 of the 242 intact unions**
:::

::: {.fragment .claimcard}
"the data for 1995 in the OSF dataset seemed faulty, since there were only 4 marriages on record (in the paper 340 marriages were listed for 1995). I thus decided to exclude the 1995 data from the analysis." <span class="src">456AP, Task 2 of Multi100</span>
:::

::: notes
How did it happen? From 2002 the survey releases a ready-made measure of how long the first cohabitation lasted. The 1995 file has none, so the length has to be built by hand from the dates in the history of each woman. The team that built the file used the month in which the woman stopped living with her first former partner, minus the month in which she began. That is a sensible rule, and it is right for almost every union that dissolved. But a stop date exists only for a partner with whom she no longer lived, so a union that became a marriage, or was still going, gets no length and drops out. Three hundred and forty-six of the separations survive, two of the marriages, three of the intact unions. [advance] One analyst, 456AP, counted first, found the gap, and left 1995 out.
:::

