## The specification menu {#the-specification-menu .smaller}

::: {.scaled style="--fs: 0.5; --lh: 1.05"}
| Level | Decision | On the curve | Off the curve |
|:--|:--|:--|:--|
| 1 – Estimands | **Surveys compared** | NSFG 1995 and 2006–2010 | Four other pairs of NSFG surveys · Trend over five surveys *(another question)* |
|  | **Cohorts compared** | Window of the paper · Same window in both | – |
|  | **Time frame** | All observed months · First three years | – |
|  | **Outcome** | Marriage to the partner | Married by the interview *(a weaker answer)*; Married within three years *(a check)*; Separation *(another question)* |
|  | **Adjustment** | No adjustment · Age at the start · Age, race-ethnicity and education | As in Model 2 · As in Model 1 *(another question)*; Length of the whole union *(a weaker answer)*; Plans to marry *(not in the data)* |
|  | **Weights** | Survey weights · No weights | – |
|  | **Women and unions** | Women, first premarital unions | Men as well *(not in the data)*; Every cohabitation *(another question)* |
| 2 – Measurement | **Union histories** | Rebuilt from the NSFG | The SCORE file *(a weaker answer)* |
|  | **Education** | As in Table 1 | As in the SCORE file *(a check)* |
|  | **Very short unions** | Left out | Kept *(a check)* |
| 3 – Estimation | **Unit of analysis** | Person-months | One row per union · Test of proportions on rows *(a weaker answer)* |
|  | **Time since the start** | Months since the start · One step per year | No time term *(a weaker answer)* |
|  | **Model** | Multinomial logit · Logit · Complementary log-log | Linear probability *(a check)* |
|  | **Standard errors** | Survey design | Clustered by woman *(a check)*; Conventional *(a weaker answer)* |
|  | **Effect scale** | Log scale | Odds or hazard ratio · Partial correlation *(a check)* |
:::

::: {.small}
Six decisions keep more than one option, and they cross into the **144** specifications of the curve. The [decision map](../cases/cohabitation/map.html) gives the reason for every option.
:::

::: notes
Here is the menu. It is the full list of decisions that any analyst of this claim has to take, fifteen of them, at three levels. Level 1 decides which question is asked: the surveys and cohorts compared, the time frame, what counts as progress to marriage, what is held constant, the weights, and the women and unions. Level 2 decides how the variables are measured, and its first row is the one that you have just met: the union histories, rebuilt from the survey or taken from the file of the analysts, which stays off the curve. Level 3 decides how the answer is reached: the unit of analysis, the time since the start, the model, the standard errors and the scale. Every option on the right was considered and left off the curve for a stated reason, which the decision map gives.
:::

## The curve of 144 {#the-curve-of-144 .smaller}

::: {.small}
- **Cohorts** – the window of the paper · the same five-year window in both surveys
- **Time frame** – all observed months · the first three years
- **Adjustment** – none · the age at the start · age, race-ethnicity and education
- **Weights** – the weights of the surveys · none
- **Time since the start** – the months as a straight line · one step per year of the union
- **Model** – multinomial logit · logit · complementary log-log
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
2 × 2 × 3 × 2 × 2 × 3 = **144**. The reference specification is the model of the paper without its interaction: **−0.730** on the log scale (a ratio of 0.48), *t* = −6.55 on 241 df with errors that follow the design of both surveys, on 33,197 person-months.

```{.r}
try_spec(cohorts = "paper", horizon = "five_years", adjust = "composition",
         weights = "weighted", time = "linear", model = "mlogit")
```
:::

::: notes
Six of the fifteen decisions keep more than one option on the curve. The cohorts: the windows of the paper, or the same five-year rule in both surveys. The time frame: every month that the data hold, or the first three years. The adjustment, three nested sets, as the graph of this morning set out. The weights, with or without. The time since the start, as a straight line in the months or a step for each year. And the model, three ways. [advance] Two times two times three times two times two times three is a hundred and forty-four specifications, and the course has fitted every one. The reference is the model of the paper without its interaction, minus nought point seven three, a ratio of nought point four eight. Every argument of try underscore spec defaults to it, so changing one thing means changing one argument.
:::

## Ground rules for the menu {#ground-rules-for-the-menu .smaller}

::: {.small}
- **Change one decision at a time**, and hold the others at the reference specification. When a result moves, you can then say what moved it
- **An option off the curve is refused** by `try_spec()`, with the reason from the menu and the page that teaches it. Asking for one, such as `histories = "score_file"` or `adjust = "race_period"`, is part of the exercise
- **One sign convention:** every estimate is the coefficient of the later cohort on the log scale, so a value **below zero** is the direction of the claim
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
The estimand statement on the [decision map](../cases/cohabitation/map.html) rewrites itself as you choose. A change in a bold phrase asks another question, a change in an italic phrase of the first sentence measures the same question differently, and a change in the second sentence reaches the answer another way.
:::

::: notes
Three ground rules before you touch the menu. First, change one decision at a time and hold the others at the reference. That sounds restrictive, but it is what makes the class chart readable, because when a result sits somewhere surprising we can say what put it there. Second, if you ask for an option that is off the curve, try underscore spec refuses it, tells you why, and names the page that teaches it. Ask for the file of the analysts, or for Model 2, and read the refusal, because the refusal is where the reasoning lives. Third, one sign convention binds everybody: every estimate is the coefficient of the later cohort on the log scale, so below zero is the direction of the claim. [advance] And the decision map on the site rewrites the estimand statement as you choose.
:::

## Weights and the survey design {#weights-and-the-survey-design .smaller}

::: {.small}
- **With the weights** of each survey the estimate describes the women aged 15–44 whom the survey represents; **without them**, the sample, in which the groups that the NSFG sampled at higher rates count for more
- The file of the analysts had **no weights**, so every Multi100 analysis and the SCORE replication are unweighted. Leaving the weights out moves the estimate by **−0.049** on average
:::

::: {.fragment .fs-75}
| The reference specification, under | SE | Statistic | df |
|:--|--:|--:|--:|
| Errors from the strata and clusters of both surveys (the curve) | 0.111 | *t* = −6.55 | 241 |
| Errors clustered by woman | 0.108 | *t* = −6.74 | 2,070 |
| Conventional errors | 0.085 | *z* = −8.56 | – |

The paper gives SE 0.14 for Model 2; the refit with design-based errors gives 0.138, with conventional errors 0.100.
:::

::: notes
Two decisions about the survey itself. The weights decide whose unions count for how much, and so which population the estimate describes. The survey sampled Black and Hispanic women at higher rates than others, so without the weights they count for more than their share of the population. The paper weights; the file of the analysts had no weights, so every Multi100 analysis describes the sample. Leaving them out makes the decline about five hundredths larger. [advance] And the standard errors. The survey drew clusters of households within strata, and the months of one union are not independent either. Conventional errors come out about a quarter smaller than errors that follow the design. The published error of nought point one four matches the design-based refit, not the conventional one, which suggests that the OAs used the design.
:::

## The length of the whole union {#the-length-of-the-whole-union .smaller}

::: {.small}
- The file of the analysts carries **COH1DUR**, the total length of each union, in every one of its months
- The SCORE replication, 73FDT, N8ZYW and JDA15 hold it constant
- But the length of a union is known only **once it has ended**, and it depends on **how** it ended: holding it constant compares unions by their outcome
:::

::: {.fragment .fs-80}
| Holding the length of the whole union constant | Without | With |
|:--|--:|--:|
| The reference specification | −0.730 | −1.104 |
| Model 2 of the paper, refitted | −0.686 | −0.608 |
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
On the menu it is a **weaker answer**: in the causal graph, the length of the union lies downstream of the outcome.
:::

::: notes
The second decision is one that the file invites. Next to every month of a union, the file carries the total length of that union, and four of the analyses of this claim held it constant, among them the replication of SCORE. It looks like a harmless control for time. It is not, because the length of a union is known only once the union has ended, and how long it lasted depends on how it ended. A union that became a marriage after six months has a recorded length of six months because the marriage ended it. Holding the length constant therefore compares unions that already share part of their outcome. [advance] At the reference settings it pushes the coefficient from minus nought point seven three to minus one point one. [advance] On the menu it stays off the curve, as a weaker answer.
:::

