## What the curve leaves out {#what-the-curve-leaves-out .smaller}

::: {.small}
- **The dependence.** All 144 specifications use the same unions, so they share their sampling noise: 144 of 144 significant **describes** the curve, and is not 144 independent confirmations
- **The data-building choices.** Every specification uses the unions rebuilt by the course. The decision that reversed the Multi100 results, the union histories, sits upstream of the curve, and a curve drawn on the file of the analysts would look just as tidy
- **The options off the curve**, and the specifications that nobody ran
- **The explanation.** The curve measures a change between two populations of unions; it cannot say whether ideas or resources produced it
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
A curve is a **map of the menu you wrote**, and it cannot show whether that menu was the right one.
:::

::: notes
Four things are missing from the curve, and a chart as tidy as ours makes them easy to forget. First, the dependence: all hundred and forty-four use the same unions, so if sampling luck favoured the claim, it favoured every specification at once. Second, everything upstream of the data. Every specification uses the rebuilt unions, and the one decision that reversed the results of Multi100 lies before any of them. A curve drawn on the file of the analysts would have looked just as tidy, and every one of its specifications that used 1995 would have been wrong. Third, the options left off on purpose, and analyses that nobody wrote down. And fourth, the explanation: the paper wanted to tell ideas from resources, and no specification of this claim can. [advance] A curve is a map of the menu you wrote, and it cannot show whether the menu was right.
:::

## The SCORE replication {#the-score-replication .smaller}

::: {.fs-75}
| Analysis of SCORE, on its own file, unweighted | Surveys | Some college × period | *z* | *p* |
|:--|:--|--:|--:|--:|
| The "reproduction dataset" | 2002 and 2006–2010 | −0.779 | −3.03 | 0.0025 |
| The "replication dataset" | 2002 and 2011–2015 | −0.365 | −1.62 | 0.106 |
:::

::: {.fragment .small}
- The criterion: "The coefficient SOMECOLLEGE_PERIOD is negative", with "p value is less than 0.05, two tailed". The verdict: "The claim was not replicated in the 'replication dataset'."
- SCORE tested the **interaction of some college and period** of Model 1, the coefficient that the Multi100 instruction excluded, with the total length of the union as a control
- The report prints p = 0.02484 for the "reproduction dataset"; its own script gives 0.0025
:::

::: {.src}
osf.io/u8x9s, the report and the script of the replication; cases/cohabitation/data/reference_marks.csv, the course refit of the script.
:::

::: notes
SCORE commissioned a replication of this paper on later waves of the same survey, and its team built the person-month file that the analysts later received. Its script fits Model 1 of the paper, without weights, on its own file. What its report calls the reproduction dataset compares 2002 with 2006 to 2010, rather than 1995, and what it calls the replication dataset compares 2002 with the waves of 2011 to 2015. [advance] The coefficient it tested is the interaction of some college and period, the one that the Multi100 instruction told the analysts to leave out. On the new waves it came out at about half its published size, with a p of nought point one one: not replicated. The report also prints a p-value ten times larger than its own script gives, a slip of the kind that only a public script lets anyone find.
:::

